        {
          "id": "lt17-m1-talk2",
          "title": "Listen to part of a lecture.",
          "audio": "/audio/listening/test-17/m1-talk2.mp3",
          "image": "/img/listening/announcement/male-2.webp",
          "speakerGender": "male",
          "transcript": "Today I want to take up a question that sounds simple and turns out not to be. Why do so many songbirds, thrushes, warblers, sparrows, do their long-distance traveling in the dark?\n\nLet's start with the air itself. During the day the sun heats the ground unevenly, and you get rising columns of warm air, turbulence, bumpy conditions. After sunset that turbulence dies down and the air goes smooth. For a small bird beating its wings continuously for eight hours, smooth air means less wasted energy.\n\nSecond, and this is the one that surprises people, there's the heat problem. Consider a warbler weighing about twelve grams. A body that small has an enormous surface area relative to its volume, and flight muscles generate a lot of heat. In daytime air the bird has to pant to dump that heat, and panting costs water it can't replace in flight. Cooler night air lets it hold onto that water.\n\nThird, predators. Falcons and hawks hunt by sight, and they're daytime hunters. Migrating at night removes most of that risk.\n\nAnd fourth, navigation. Here's the point that gets misunderstood constantly. Students assume the stars are just a backup for cloudy magnetic sensing. They're not. Young birds learn the rotation of the night sky around the pole star, and that gives them a reliable reference for north.\n\nSo we're looking at a behavior that's overdetermined. Cool air, water savings, safety, and a usable compass all pointing the same direction.",
          "questions": [
            {
              "id": "lt17-m1-talk2-q1",
              "stem": "What is the main point the lecturer makes about nocturnal migration?",
              "options": [
                {
                  "text": "It occurs because songbirds physically cannot generate lift in warm daytime air",
                  "correct": false,
                  "id": "a"
                },
                {
                  "text": "It's a recent behavior that appeared as daytime predators became more numerous",
                  "correct": false,
                  "id": "b"
                },
                {
                  "text": "It results from several separate advantages that happen to reinforce each other",
                  "correct": true,
                  "id": "c"
                },
                {
                  "text": "It's mainly a way for small birds to avoid competing with larger daytime migrants",
                  "correct": false,
                  "id": "d"
                }
              ],
              "explanation": "The lecturer lists cooler stable air, water conservation, safety from hawks, and star-based orientation, then says the behavior is overdetermined, meaning several advantages stack up. The other options are never claimed: no claim about recency, no claim that lift is impossible, and no discussion of competition with larger migrants."
            },
            {
              "id": "lt17-m1-talk2-q2",
              "stem": "According to the lecturer, what happens to the atmosphere after sunset?",
              "options": [
                {
                  "text": "Thermal columns strengthen and lift birds to higher altitudes",
                  "correct": false,
                  "id": "a"
                },
                {
                  "text": "Humidity drops sharply, which speeds up water loss",
                  "correct": false,
                  "id": "b"
                },
                {
                  "text": "Turbulence dies down and the air becomes smoother",
                  "correct": true,
                  "id": "c"
                },
                {
                  "text": "Winds reverse direction and blow steadily southward",
                  "correct": false,
                  "id": "d"
                }
              ],
              "explanation": "The lecturer says the turbulence generated by daytime heating dies down and the air goes smooth. Wind reversal and stronger thermals are not mentioned, and cooler night air is described as reducing water loss, not humidity dropping."
            },
            {
              "id": "lt17-m1-talk2-q3",
              "stem": "Why does the lecturer describe the small warbler weighing about twelve grams?",
              "options": [
                {
                  "text": "To demonstrate that lighter birds can fly farther than heavier ones",
                  "correct": false,
                  "id": "a"
                },
                {
                  "text": "To give a concrete case showing why heat and water loss matter so much",
                  "correct": true,
                  "id": "b"
                },
                {
                  "text": "To explain how researchers weigh birds captured at banding stations",
                  "correct": false,
                  "id": "c"
                },
                {
                  "text": "To show that warblers are the only family that migrates after dark",
                  "correct": false,
                  "id": "d"
                }
              ],
              "explanation": "The warbler example illustrates the surface-area-to-volume problem: a tiny body overheats and dehydrates fast, so flying at night saves water. It isn't used to compare distances, to claim exclusivity for warblers, or to describe banding methods."
            },
            {
              "id": "lt17-m1-talk2-q4",
              "stem": "What can be inferred about a night-migrating bird taken into a planetarium?",
              "options": [
                {
                  "text": "Its heading can be shifted by changing the projected sky",
                  "correct": true,
                  "id": "a"
                },
                {
                  "text": "It will refuse to orient until natural starlight returns",
                  "correct": false,
                  "id": "b"
                },
                {
                  "text": "It orients correctly only if the magnetic field is also altered",
                  "correct": false,
                  "id": "c"
                },
                {
                  "text": "It relies on the moon rather than on any pattern of stars",
                  "correct": false,
                  "id": "d"
                }
              ],
              "explanation": "The lecturer says birds read the rotation of the sky around the pole star, so an artificial sky provides the same cue and redirecting it would redirect the bird. The bird does orient indoors, it uses star rotation rather than the moon, and no claim ties correct orientation to altering the magnetic field."
            }
          ]
        }
