          "explanation": "The speaker's point is that no lab coats can be bought at the bookstore for about a week, so a listener who understands this needs another way to get a coat in the meantime, which is exactly what \"borrow my roommate's coat\" does: it accepts the bad news and offers a practical solution for the upcoming lab session. The reply \"go buy one there this afternoon\" is impossible given what was just said, since the whole message was that the store has none in stock; agreeing with \"Great\" and then planning a purchase shows the phrase \"out of\" was missed entirely. The reply \"the chemistry lab is on the third floor\" answers a question about location that nobody asked; it latches onto the word \"lab\" and gives directions instead of responding to the shortage of coats. The reply \"textbook prices there are ridiculous\" reacts to the word \"bookstore\" rather than to the meaning of the sentence, switching the topic to cost when the speaker said nothing about price, only about availability. Listen for the core problem in a statement, then choose the response that deals with that problem.",
