        "explanation": "The speaker is passing along a practical piece of news: the bus stop has been relocated across the street, beside the science building. A good response shows that the listener understood the news and recognized why it matters to them. \"Good thing you told me, I would have waited on the old corner\" does exactly that, acknowledging the new information and drawing the natural conclusion that the listener would otherwise have gone to the wrong spot. The reply \"No, the science building is definitely on this side of the street\" contradicts something the speaker never claimed; the speaker located the stop, not the building, so this response disputes a point that was not at issue and is inconsistent with the information just given. \"Sure, I can drive you home tonight if the bus is full\" answers a request that was never made; nobody mentioned a crowded bus or asked for a ride, so it responds to an imagined question instead of the statement about the stop's new location. \"I know, the street festival downtown was crowded all weekend\" latches onto the word \"street\" and drifts into an unrelated topic, reacting to a single word rather than to the meaning of the whole sentence.",
