        "image": "/img/listening/announcement/female-1.webp",
        "speakerGender": "female",
        "transcript": "Today I want to look at one of the most remarkable communication systems outside of human language: the honeybee waggle dance.\n\nSo here's the situation. A forager finds a patch of flowers half a mile away. She comes back to a dark, crowded hive, and somehow her sisters fly straight out to that same patch. How? She dances.\n\nLet's break the dance into two pieces. First, duration. The bee runs forward while vibrating her body, and the length of that waggle run tracks distance. Roughly one second of waggling corresponds to about a kilometer of flight. Second, angle. And this is the elegant part. Inside the hive, on a vertical comb, straight up means fly toward the sun. So if the dancer runs at forty degrees to the right of vertical, the followers should leave the hive and fly forty degrees to the right of the sun's direction. That's the whole code.\n\nNow, here's the point students most often get wrong. People assume the dance gives an exact address, like a street number. It doesn't. It gives an approximation, and the followers still search the area using odor cues once they arrive. The dance narrows the search dramatically, but it doesn't eliminate it.\n\nOne more thing worth noting. The sun moves across the sky during the day. Bees compensate using an internal clock, so a dance performed at noon and remembered an hour later gets adjusted. That flexibility tells us this isn't a rigid reflex. It's a genuine translation between two different frames of reference.",
